WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.440
A monitoring baseline is a reference for expected assets, communication paths, protocols,

00:00:05.440 --> 00:00:07.360
roles, timing, and operational behavior.

00:00:07.360 --> 00:00:09.660
OT networks often have repeatable patterns,

00:00:09.660 --> 00:00:13.230
which makes meaningful changes visible when the baseline is accurate and maintained.

00:00:13.230 --> 00:00:15.255
Start with inventory and architecture.

00:00:15.255 --> 00:00:19.690
Identify expected devices, their function, normal peers, approved remote connections,

00:00:19.690 --> 00:00:21.790
engineering pathways, and trust boundaries.

00:00:21.790 --> 00:00:25.630
Observe several operating conditions, including startup, shutdown, maintenance,

00:00:25.630 --> 00:00:28.850
batch changes, failover, and seasonal or production variation.

00:00:28.850 --> 00:00:33.510
Anomalies may include a new device, a new communication pair, an unexpected protocol,

00:00:33.510 --> 00:00:36.590
activity outside an approved window, changed message frequency,

00:00:36.590 --> 00:00:40.640
unusual engineering access, or a command pattern that differs from the normal role.

00:00:40.640 --> 00:00:44.660
A change in behavior is evidence for investigation, not proof of malicious activity.

00:00:44.660 --> 00:00:47.360
Process faults, equipment replacement, troubleshooting,

00:00:47.360 --> 00:00:49.955
and authorized maintenance can all create anomalies.

00:00:49.955 --> 00:00:54.160
Operators and engineers can explain whether the observed activity matches the process and

00:00:54.160 --> 00:00:54.740
work plan.

00:00:54.740 --> 00:00:58.795
Monitoring teams should capture that context so the same approved pattern does not create

00:00:58.795 --> 00:00:59.740
repeated noise.

00:00:59.740 --> 00:01:04.320
Baselines can also preserve unsafe behavior if the learning period contains unmanaged

00:01:04.320 --> 00:01:05.799
access or misconfiguration.

00:01:05.799 --> 00:01:08.800
Compare observed patterns with approved architecture and policy.

00:01:08.800 --> 00:01:11.030
Known normal is not automatically known good.

00:01:11.030 --> 00:01:12.990
Update baselines through change control.

00:01:12.990 --> 00:01:17.520
Document new equipment, firmware, production paths, vendor access, and expected timing.

00:01:17.520 --> 00:01:19.120
Tune detection using evidence,

00:01:19.120 --> 00:01:22.405
but avoid suppressing entire categories simply because they are noisy.

00:01:22.405 --> 00:01:26.350
Effective anomaly detection balances sensitivity with operational understanding,

00:01:26.350 --> 00:01:29.920
producing a manageable set of changes that knowledgeable teams can investigate.
